    "body": "Heavier shipments are driving higher tonnage and revenue per bill at ArcBest’s less-than-truckload unit. Freight lost to a once-depressed truckload market is returning alongside an improved manufacturing complex. The changing freight mix helped the company beat third-quarter expectations on Wednesday.\nThe Institute for Supply Management’s Manufacturing PMI remained in expansion territory for a 10th consecutive month in October. The 55.5 reading was 100 basis points above expectations and the September mark. (A reading above 50 signals expansion, while one below 50 indicates contraction.)\nArcBest ( NASDAQ: ARCB ) reported adjusted earnings per share of $2.62 on Wednesday before the market opened. The result was 79% higher year over year and 11 cents better than the consensus estimate. (The adjusted number excluded restructuring, technology and other nonrecurring costs.)\nConsolidated revenue was up 15% y/y to $1.21 billion, in line with consensus.\nTable: ArcBest’s key performance indicators\nLTL: Q3 by the numbers\nThe company’s asset-based unit, which includes LTL subsidiary ABF Freight, reported revenue of $799 million, a 10% y/y increase. Tonnage was up 10% y/y as shipments declined 3% and weight per shipment jumped 13%. On a y/y comparison, tonnage increased 8.1% in July, 9.1% in August and 11.6% in September.\nRevenue per hundredweight was 1% higher y/y (inclusive of fuel surcharges) and revenue per shipment was 14% higher, with both metrics being impacted by the higher shipment weights. Excluding fuel, yield dropped by a low single-digit percentage in the period, dragged down by heavier shipment weights and a 2% shorter average length of haul.\nContractual rate increases averaged 5.1% in the quarter (up 9.6% on a two-year-stacked comparison). ArcBest implemented a 5.9% general rate increase for LTL services in both of its business units in June.\nA 90.4% adjusted operating ratio (inverse of operating margin ) was 210 bps better y/y. The result was 40 bps better than the second quarter and management’s outlook for no material sequential change.\nArcBest said it reduced its base liability insurance premiums while maintaining coverage limits during a recent renewal. However, it has retained higher risk on one of its excess insurance layers; that coverage has only been touched twice over the past 25 years.\nThe period also had the impact from an annual union wage increase.\nQ4 asset-based outlook\nRevenue per day was up 20% y/y in October as tonnage increased 15% and yield increased 4%. (Shipments were up 1% with revenue per shipment up 18%). October tonnage was down only 1% from September. It’s normally down 3.4%. Yield (ex-fuel) was again off by a low-single-digit percentage y/y, largely due to a 14% increase in weight per shipment.\nIt noted that weight per shipment growth will begin to moderate as the prior-year comparisons get tougher. It will continue to lean on its dynamic quote tool, which matches available network capacity to available transactional shipments in the market each day.\nThe unit’s OR normally deteriorates by 180 bps from the third to the fourth quarter, but management expects just 50 to 150 bps of erosion this year. The implied 91.4% OR guide (at the midpoint of the range) would be 480 bps better y/y. It said 2027 will likely be another positive step on its path to achieve 2028 financial targets .\nAsset-light beats raised expectations\nThe company’s asset-light segment, which includes truck brokerage, beat raised expectations. Adjusted operating income of $14.4 million in the third quarter was ahead of the revised guidance ($10 million to $12 million) management provided in its August update . (The initial guide for the unit was $6 million to $8 million.)\nRevenue of $446 million was 25% higher y/y as shipments increased 1% and revenue per shipment jumped 24% (including fuel). Purchased transportation expense (as a percentage of revenue) was up 10 bps y/y to 85%. Shipments per person per day increased 8% and selling, general and administrative expenses declined 2%.\nThe trends carried through October, with revenue per day 27% higher y/y (shipments up 8%, yield up 18%). Adjusted operating income is expected to range from $7 million to $10 million in the fourth quarter.\nWhy it matters? ArcBest is one of a few publicly traded LTL companies. Its quarterly results provide insight into a subsegment of trucking where few public datasets exist.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nPeak season inventory surge bolsters TL carriers’ double-digit rate ask\nPamt Corp. reports Q3 net loss, books 109.1% truckload OR\nOctober’s 55.5 manufacturing PMI beats expectations",
